        <tr><td>Easy coding entry</td><td><strong>Best</strong></td><td>Very good</td><td>Overkill</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open-source maker focus</td><td>Good</td><td><strong>Best fit</strong></td><td>Advanced / ROS focused</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scratch / Python / Arduino</td><td><strong>Strong</strong></td><td>Strong</td><td>Different, more advanced stack</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI vision / navigation</td><td>Entry-level camera features</td><td>Expandable</td><td><strong>Best</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lowest observed entry price</td><td><strong>$299.99</strong></td><td>$319</td><td>$719.99 advanced configuration checked</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="warning-grid">
      <div class="section-head"><p class="section-kicker">Before Christmas morning</p><h2>Five things robot-dog listings rarely make clear</h2></div>
      <div class="warnings">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Pet, toy, project or companion?</h3><p>Bittle can be brilliant for a tinkerer and disappointing for somebody who wanted affection. A plush senior companion dog solves a different problem again.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>How much setup?</h3><p>Remote-control toys start quickly. Programmable quadrupeds may need software, Wi-Fi, coding tools and patience.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Does the fun survive without an app?</h3><p>Connected features are useful, but buyers should know what remains if the app, account or service changes.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Battery and charging</h3><p>Autonomous AI features consume power. A robot that repeatedly returns to a dock behaves very differently from a simple battery toy.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Do not buy specs nobody will use</h3><p>ROS, LiDAR and large-model integrations are valuable only when the recipient actually wants to experiment with them.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq">
